course: dictionary
lesson: 10087
title: Linking words that show a reason
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words join two ideas together. Therefore is one of them: it tells you that something follows from what came before. «12 is even; therefore it can be split into two equal groups.» The first part gives the reason, and the part after therefore is the result.
explain: You can often say so or for that reason instead of therefore. Read both versions: «It is raining, so we will stay in» and «It is raining; therefore we will stay in» mean the same thing. The second one sounds more formal, like a science book or an exam answer.
explain: In maths you may see the symbol ∴, which also means therefore. In speech people usually say so, but in writing you will often find therefore. Look out for phrases such as and therefore, it is therefore, and therefore we can say.
fact: Therefore means so, or for that reason.
fact: Therefore shows that something follows from what was said before.
fact: «I think, therefore I am» is a famous saying by René Descartes, a French philosopher of the 1600s.
fact: The symbol ∴ in maths means therefore.
fact: In speech people usually say so; therefore is more common in writing, especially in formal texts and exams.
exercise: «12 is even; therefore it can be split into two equal groups.» Which word could replace therefore with the same meaning? | but | although | *so | before
exercise: Which symbol in maths means therefore? | ÷ | *∴ | % | ∞
exercise: Which word means so, or for that reason? | quickly | *therefore | blue | happily
test: In «I think, therefore I am», what does therefore link? | Two words that mean the opposite | Two questions that have no answer | *The reason, I think, with the result, I am | A list of things Descartes liked
test: Which sentence means the same as «It is raining, so we will stay in»? | We will stay in; it was sunny | It stopped raining, but we went out | *It is raining; therefore we will stay in | It is raining; therefore we went out
exam: Rewrite using therefore: «The bag was heavy. We took a taxi.» | We took a taxi; therefore the bag was light | The bag was heavy; therefore we walked home | *The bag was heavy; therefore we took a taxi | The bag was heavy, but we walked
